Topi Linkala wrote:
> nikolai kingsley wrote:
> > The Shrewd Dude wrote:
>
> >> The #enhancement of combat skills is determined by how often you use
> >> said weapon, not by what experience level you are. Just because you've
> >> been raising levels using a guisarme, for example, doesn't mean that
> >> you're get any more skilled in quarterstaff.

Skills classes/schools come ready for enhancement when a certain
number of successfull hist (2+ damage points) or casts have been done
in that class.

So it isn't about how often you use a weapon really.  It's about how
often you actually hit with it and with other similar weapons.  The way
weapons are divided into classes is far from obvious in some cases -
scimitar and saber are different classes, crysknife is knife not
dagger,
athame is dagger not knife and so on.  For spell skills the attack ones
seem easy to figure, but other schools can same random until you
spend several games casting spells.

It also takes having a slot available.  Early in games I tend to level
up faster than I can practice weapons so I rarely have any pending
enhancements.  Later in games leveling up becomes rare so I
often have several offered.  "You feel more confident in your skills"
and when I look there are several lettered choices.

> > is there a point in giving the player the choice of #enhancing or not?
>
> As there are only 29 skill slots that the player can use to enhance his
> skills compared to hundreds one would need if one would like to enhance
> all posiible skills, the only way is to let the player select what he
> wants to enhance.
>
> People don't want to use those skill slots for something just because
> they can. I would hate if the game would enhance my wizards quarterstaff
> skill.

This makes managing weapons-class/spell-school slots yet another
skill to be developed as you play Nethack.  Early on it's running away.
By the time you're exploring the mines it's dropping stuff in stashes.
By the time you're facing Medusa it's managing your skill slots.



